LL's generator writes each event's rule in their LSL compiler's grammar:
the event's identifiers, each made and handed to gAllocationManager, which
deletes what it holds when a compile ends, and then the event itself, made
and handed to nothing. The rules the grammar writes by hand hand the event
over too. So every event a script handles was left behind by each compile,
with what it held, as LeakSanitizer reports. The generated rule hands the
event to gAllocationManager as well.

diff --git a/lsl_definitions/python/lsl_definitions/generators/lscript_compiler.py b/lsl_definitions/python/lsl_definitions/generators/lscript_compiler.py
index 357f2d4..76b97da 100644
--- a/lsl_definitions/python/lsl_definitions/generators/lscript_compiler.py
+++ b/lsl_definitions/python/lsl_definitions/generators/lscript_compiler.py
@@ -266,6 +266,7 @@ def gen_parser_file(definitions: LSLDefinitions, template_path: str) -> str:
                 arg_str = ", " + arg_str
             generated_event_definitions += (
                 f"        $$ = new LLScript{event_class}Event(gLine, gColumn{arg_str});\n"
+                "        gAllocationManager->addAllocation($$);\n"
             )
 
             generated_event_definitions += "    }\n    ;\n\n"
